      "text": "Let's begin first with the facts.\nThe \"bottom\" of this figure is facing you, the upside-down triangle is the bottom's base, and the upside-down circle is easily seen to be in the center.\nBecause the small triangles lie on the sides of each other, we can conclude that each of them must be congruent.\nWe can also figure out the measures of $\\angle{BAD}$ and $\\angle{EDC}$, I believe that these are given in the figure.\nFrom what I've surmised, I now know that $AB = BC = AC$.\n\nI would now bring in another fact, which is that each of the segments are congruent to each other, so let's assume the value $9 <$ $x =$ 90$.\n\nThe angles in a $90^\\circ$ triangle sometimes look like this:\nI have made a triangle that is in the shape given to you, but I have extended the sides going out to another angle.\n\nI can now see that the $60^\\circ$ from the left has to be on each of the right angles, so $\\angle{EAD}$ and $\\angle{CAE}$ are equal.\nOhh, wait, $\\angle{CAE}$ I erased! Oh well\n\nWith what we've done so far, we should be able to see how to continue...\n\nWe now move to another method, which was easier; the triangle. \nNow, we can now figure a number of different triangles inside of this larger triangle. Indeed, I will begin doing that:\n\nThe big triangle contains $55^\\circ$ angles, two and $x^\\circ$ angles, let $y$ be the measure of the angle I am trying to find $\\ \n\\angle{ADO}$. \n\nWe can now see two smaller triangles and one shared triangle. It turns out that $40^\\circ + y = 30^\\circ + 35^\\circ = 65^\\circ = x$.\n\nI now solve this equation to find that $y = 115^\\circ$\n\nThus, the longest segment is in the question, which I'll label $x$, and longest is really $x = \\boxed{115}$.\n\n![enter image description here](https://i.imgur.com/DaxDMwe.png)\n\n$\\boxed{115}$",
