      "text": "[asy]\npathpen = black;\npointpen = black;\n\npair A = (-3,0), B=(0,2), C=(3,0), D=(0,-1);\ndraw(D(MP(\"A\", A, W))--D(MP(\"B\", B, N))--D(MP(\"C\", C, E))--D(MP(\"D\", D, S))--A);\ndraw(B--D);\nlabel(\"A\",A,NW);\nlabel(\"B\",B,SW);\nlabel(\"C\",C,SE);\nlabel(\"D\",D,NE);\n\npair Z=IP((0,-1)..C--(-3,0)..D,((0,0)..B--C..(0,1)));\ndraw(Circle((Z),1));\npair S=foot((0,-1),(0,1),Z), T=foot(B,(0,2),Z);\ndraw(Z--S);\ndraw(Z--T);\nreal x = X(Z,(0,-1))/_p((0,1));\nlabel(\"X\",(0,1),NE,fontsize(10*x));\npair Y=X((0,1),Z)/_p((0,-1));\ndraw((0,1)--Y--Z,Arrows(size=5));\nMarkInterval((0,-1),B,C,Z,0,1);\nMarkInterval((0,1),B,C,Z,1,2);\npair F=IP((0,1)..D..cycle,((0,-1)..B..(0,0)));\ndraw((0,1)--F--Z);\npair P = IP(circumcircle(B, C, D), B--F);\ndraw((A--B)--(B--C)--(C--D)--(D--A));\nmarker(_,\"A\", dir(135),ellipse((135,0),0.07));\npair ib = incenter(A,B,C); dot(ib);\ndraw(rightanglemark(C,ib,A,1));\ndraw(rightanglemark(ib,B,ib,C));\nmarker(_,\"B\", dir(135),ellipse((135,0),0.07));\npair jb= incenter(B,C,D); dot(jb);\ndraw(leftanglemark(D,jb,B,1));\ndraw(leftanglemark(jb,C,jb,D));\ndot((107.29,-1));\npair pf = extension( circumcircle(A,B,C), circumcircle(B,C,D), Y, Z ); dot(pf);\nlabel(rotate(90)*\"Longest\",pf,N);\n[/asy] To solve this problem, we need to understand the angles and the properties of the angles formed in the figure.\n\nGiven the information, we know the following angles:\n- Angle \\( \\angle BAD = 55^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( \\angle ABC = 55^\\circ \\)\n- Angle \\( \\angle BCD = 75^\\circ \\)\n\nFrom these angles, we can infer:\n- \\( \\angle DAB = 55^\\circ \\) because \\( \\angle BAD = 55^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle DAB \\) is vertically opposite to \\( \\angle BAD \\).\n- \\( \\angle CAD = 180^\\circ - 55^\\circ - 55^\\circ = 60^\\circ \\) because the angles around point \\( A \\) sum to \\( 360^\\circ \\).\n- \\( \\angle ABD = 55^\\circ \\) because \\( \\angle BAD = 55^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle ADB = 40^\\circ \\) (from the vertical angle).\n\nNow, let's consider the pentagon \\( ABCDE \\). In a pentagon, the sum of the interior angles is \\( (5-2) \\times 180^\\circ = 540^\\circ \\). We have already calculated the angles \\( \\angle BAC = 60^\\circ \\), \\( \\angle BCD = 75^\\circ \\), and \\( \\angle CAD = 60^\\circ \\). Therefore, the missing angle is:\n\\[ \\text{Angle } AEB = 540^\\circ - (60^\\circ + 75^\\circ + 60^\\circ + 55^\\circ) = 150^\\circ \\]\n\nNow, let's denote the length of segment \\( AE \\) as \\( x \\) and the length of segment \\( EC \\) as \\( y \\). We need to find which of these segments is the longest.\n\nWe know that \\( \\angle AEB = 150^\\circ \\). In the triangle \\( AEB \\), we can use the Law of Sines:\n\\[ \\frac{AE}{\\sin \\angle BEC} = \\frac{EB}{\\sin \\angle BEA} \\]\nSince \\( \\angle BEA = 180^\\circ - 150^\\circ = 30^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle BEC = 30^\\circ (since \\angle BEC = 180^\\circ - 75^\\circ - 15^\\circ) \\), we get:\n\\[ \\frac{x}{\\sin 75^\\circ} = \\frac{15}{\\sin 30^\\circ} \\]\n\\[ \\frac{x}{\\sin 75^\\circ} = \\frac{15}{0.5} = 30 \\]\n\\[ x = 30 \\sin 75^\\circ \\]\n\nSimilarly, we can find the length of \\( EC \\):\n\\[ \\frac{EC}{\\sin \\angle EDC} = \\frac{EB}{\\sin \\angle CEA} \\]\nSince \\( \\angle CEA = 30^\\circ \\) and \\( \\angle EDC = 75^\\circ \\), we get:\n\\[ \\frac{y}{\\sin 75^\\circ} = \\frac{15}{\\sin 15^\\circ} \\]\n\\[ y = 15 \\sin 75^\\circ \\tan 15^\\circ \\]\n\nNow, compare \\( x \\) and \\( y \\):\n\\[ x = 30 \\sin 75^\\circ \\]\n\\[ y = 15 \\sin 75^\\circ \\tan 15^\\circ \\]\n\nUsing the double-angle identity for sine, \\( \\sin 75^\\circ = \\sin (45^\\circ + 30^\\circ) = \\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\), and the tangent identity \\( \\tan 15^\\circ = \\frac{\\sin 15^\\circ}{\\cos 15^\\circ} = \\frac{2 - \\sqrt{3}}{2 + \\sqrt{3}} = \\frac{-1 + \\sqrt{3}}{1 + \\sqrt{3}} \\approx 0.766 \\):\n\n\\[ y = 15 \\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{2}}{4} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sqrt{3}}{2} \\approx 15 \\cdot 0.5805 \\cdot 0.268 = 2.34 \\]\n\nSo, the longest segment is \\( AE \\). The answer is:\n\\[ \\boxed{AE} \\]",
